  "worked_solution": "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u20612\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}\u20612\\theta =2{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{6},$ we have\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{x}{3})=2{sin}^{2}\u2061(\\frac{x}{6}).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3\\pi }^{6\\pi }6{sin}^{2}\u2061(\\frac{x}{6})\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{3\\pi }^{6\\pi }2{sin}^{2}\u2061(\\frac{x}{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{3\\pi }^{6\\pi }[1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{x}{3})]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{[x-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{x}{3})]}_{3\\pi }^{6\\pi } \\\\  & =3[6\\pi -3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{6\\pi }{3})-(3\\pi -3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{3\\pi }{3}))] \\\\  & =3[6\\pi -3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\pi )-(3\\pi -3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi ))] \\\\  & =3(6\\pi -3\\pi ) \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
